{
 "title": "Saanjh Ki Dor (The Twilight Thread)",
 "concept": "His phrase and her phrase become their song. The mukhda is a two-part tune in D: a rising call (Pa-Sa-Re-Ga, a small dip, then a meend leap that lands on the anticipated last eighth of beat 3, where the names appear) and a falling answer. The bansuri gives the call to the groom and the violin gives the answer to the bride; when the couple appears the two halves meet, then rise together in parallel thirds. At the stage reveal the call climbs one step higher onto komal ni (C6 over a bVII chord) with sweeping octave strings, and the resolution is coloured by Charukeshi komal dha (Bb). The card carries the motif in the relative minor with an E-minor answer. The finale brings back call and resolution, cadences bVI-bVII-I and rests on D6/9, the swaras of Bhupali, with a swarmandal glissando.",
 "meter": "12/8",
 "key": "D major (Sa = D), Khamaj / Charukeshi colours",
 "mode_notes": "Sa = D. Base scale D E F# G A B C# (shuddha). Khamaj colour: C natural (komal ni) in descents and on the bVII chord: the stage peak C6 and the Cadd9 lifts (bars 6, 10, 18). Charukeshi colour: Bb (komal dha) against shuddha Ga: Gm6 (bars 6, 16), Gm(add9)/Bb (bar 12), Bb(add9) (bar 18), and the meends B5->Bb5 (violin, bar 6) and A5->Bb5 (bansuri, bar 16). Minor side of the blend: the call restated in B minor (bar 13), Em7 / F#m7 colours. Ornaments: ~ meend, ^ kan, short murki F#-E before Sa. Final chord D6/9 = Sa Re Ga Pa Dha (Bhupali); the harp glissandi use the same pentatonic.",
